+---
+id: dl-004-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386-semantics-for-cloning-and-prototype-safety
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386) semantics for cloning and prototype safety"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 7386's algorithm is short (recurse while both sides are objects, delete on a `null` member,
+otherwise replace wholesale) but leaves the same two implementation corners open that dl-001/dl-002/
+dl-003 already settled for this library's other JSON Pointer/Patch features, plus one that is new to
+merge patch:
+
+- The reference algorithm's base case, `return Patch`, hands back a value that may still contain
+  arrays/objects reachable from the caller's `patch` argument (e.g. an array value, or a value nested
+  inside one). Returning it by reference would let the caller's later mutations of `patch` leak into
+  an already-returned result, and would let two merges of the same `patch` object alias each other's
+  output.
+- A merge patch is exactly as attacker-shaped as a JSON Patch: it is a bag of member names and values
+  parsed from client-submitted JSON, so it can contain a member literally named `__proto__`, and the
+  reference algorithm's `Target[Name] = ...` step is ordinary bracket assignment, which dl-003 already
+  found silently drops data (and never reaches the real `Object.prototype`) because `"__proto__"` is a
+  special accessor name inherited from `Object.prototype`, not an ordinary writable slot.
+- Symmetrically, the reference algorithm reads `Target[Name]` to find the current value to recurse
+  into. For a plain object with no *own* `__proto__` member, that bracket read returns the real
+  `Object.prototype` (the accessor's getter), which would then be treated as "the current value of
+  the `__proto__` member" and merged into — reachable even though dl-003's `setOwnProperty` fix means
+  nothing is ever *written* there.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyMergePatch`'s base case (`patch` is not a JSON object — a scalar, an array, or `null`)
+  returns `structuredClone(patch)`, not `patch` itself, matching dl-002's precedent that values taken
+  from a patch argument are cloned before they reach the result so the output shares no mutable
+  references with the patch input. The recursive-object case never needs its own clone of `patch`'s
+  members because every member is routed back through `applyMergePatch`, which hits this same base
+  case (or recurses further and eventually does) for every leaf.
+- Object members are written with the `setOwnProperty` helper (`Object.defineProperty`, `writable:
+  enumerable: configurable: true`) factored out of `jsonPatch.ts` into the shared `jsonPointer.ts`
+  module, instead of bracket assignment — the same fix dl-003 made for JSON Patch, reused here rather
+  than reimplemented, so a member literally named `__proto__` in the patch round-trips like any other
+  member name instead of being silently discarded.
+- Reading the target's current value for a given member name is gated by
+  `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(result, name)`, defaulting to `undefined` when it is `false`
+  — the same own-property gate `resolveToken`/`navigateToParent` already use for JSON Pointer/Patch —
+  so a non-own `__proto__` (the inherited accessor on every plain object) is treated as "this member
+  is absent", not as an existing object to merge into.
+- Deletion for a `null`-valued patch member uses plain `delete result[name]`, with no own-property
+  guard: JS's `delete` operator only ever removes an *own* property and is a harmless no-op on a
+  bracket key that resolves to an inherited accessor (like a non-own `__proto__`), so it already has
+  the right behaviour — "remove the member if it exists as a member, do nothing otherwise" — without
+  needing the explicit check `addToParent`-style code requires.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` never mutates `target` or `patch`, and the returned document
+  shares no mutable references with either argument, consistent with how `applyPatch` already
+  behaves.
+- A target or patch member literally named `__proto__` behaves like any other member name: it can be
+  set, merged into recursively, or deleted by a `null` patch value, and `Object.prototype` is never
+  reachable for mutation through `applyMergePatch`.
+- `setOwnProperty` is now a shared export of `jsonPointer.ts` used by both `jsonPatch.ts` and
+  `jsonMergePatch.ts`, rather than being private to `jsonPatch.ts`; this is a non-behavioural refactor
+  of `jsonPatch.ts` (same 57 pre-existing tests for `jsonPointer`/`jsonPatch` pass unmodified).
