@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+---
+id: task-004-implement-rfc-7386-json-merge-patch-applymergepatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement RFC 7386 JSON Merge Patch applyMergePatch"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Some clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format. Implement JSON Merge Patch
+per RFC 7386, exported as `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a merge
+patch to a target JSON document and returns the merged document.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Added `src/jsonMergePatch.ts` with `applyMergePatch(target, patch)`, implementing RFC 7386 §2's
+  recursive algorithm:
+  - If `patch` is a JSON object, each of its members is merged into `target` (coerced to `{}` first
+    if `target` isn't itself a JSON object): a `null`-valued member removes that member from the
+    result, any other value recurses (`applyMergePatch(currentValue, patchValue)`).
+  - If `patch` is not a JSON object (a scalar, an array, or `null`), it replaces `target` wholesale —
+    this is also what makes a top-level non-object patch replace the whole document, and what makes
+    an array patch member replace an array/scalar target member instead of merging element-wise.
+  - Reads the target's current value for a member via `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty`, so a
+    non-own member (notably the inherited `__proto__` accessor every plain object has) is treated as
+    absent rather than resolving to `Object.prototype` and being merged into.
+  - Writes result members through the shared `setOwnProperty` helper (`Object.defineProperty`)
+    instead of bracket assignment, so a patch member literally named `__proto__` is stored as a
+    genuine own property instead of silently invoking the inherited accessor's setter and dropping
+    the value.
+  - Clones with `structuredClone` at the two points data crosses from an argument into the result
+    (the initial `target`, and `patch`'s own base-case values), so the returned document shares no
+    mutable references with either `target` or `patch`.
+- Refactored `setOwnProperty` out of `src/jsonPatch.ts` into `src/jsonPointer.ts` (exported, with a
+  doc comment) so both `jsonPatch.ts` and the new `jsonMergePatch.ts` share one implementation;
+  `jsonPatch.ts`'s behaviour and its 57 pre-existing tests are unchanged.
+- `src/index.ts` now also re-exports `applyMergePatch`.
+- Added `src/jsonMergePatch.test.ts` (22 tests, written before the implementation: red, then green),
+  covering RFC 7386 Appendix A's worked examples (replace/add/remove members, nested-object merge,
+  array-as-scalar wholesale replacement, top-level array/scalar/`null` patches, a `null` member
+  alongside an unrelated existing `null`-valued member, merging an object patch into a non-object
+  target, deep member creation while pruning `null` leaves), no-mutation of `target` and of `patch`,
+  no aliasing between the result and either argument, and a member literally named `__proto__` being
+  set, read, and removed without ever touching `Object.prototype`.
+- Recorded the cloning and `__proto__`-handling design choices (both reusing dl-002/dl-003's
+  precedent from JSON Patch) as
+  dl-004-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386-semantics-for-cloning-and-prototype-safety.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 79/79 passing (57 pre-existing `jsonPointer`/`jsonPatch` tests unchanged + 22 new
+  `jsonMergePatch` tests).
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors.
+- Manually checked `applyMergePatch` against RFC 7386 Appendix A's full example table, including the
+  top-level `null`-patch and non-object-target cases.
